{"review_id":"00001788559361226278-0001981761-000001-cycle-review-1","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:67d81b26772e06d230dd9540a704908020d8212df256dd320f9dfd5627989b2a","findings":[{"finding_id":"F1-dangling-symlink-escape","category":"security_issue","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"canonicalize_partial treats every canonicalize error as \"does not exist\", so a dangling symlink component still escapes the worktree","claim":"`canonicalize_partial` discards the error returned by `Path::canonicalize` and unconditionally walks up to the deepest ancestor that canonicalizes, re-appending the remaining components literally. It never distinguishes ENOENT (the intended not-yet-created-leaf case) from a component that failed to resolve for another reason, and — decisively — a symlink whose target does not exist fails `canonicalize` with ENOENT exactly like a missing file. So for `apply-edit` with `path: \"victim.txt\"` where `victim.txt` is a symlink inside the worktree pointing at a non-existent path outside it (a worker can create one with `run-command`: `ln -s ~/.config/autostart/x.desktop victim.txt`), the canonicalization of `<root>/victim.txt` fails, the loop falls back to canonicalizing `<root>` and re-appending `victim.txt` literally, the resulting path starts with the canonical root, and the write is authorized. `fs::write` then follows the symlink and creates the file outside the worktree. The same fallback admits an intermediate dangling-symlink component (`link/new.txt` with `link -> /outside/dir-created-later`), and the equivalent hole exists for any other non-ENOENT failure (EACCES, ELOOP) where the fallback silently degrades back to the lexical-only check this change set out to replace. The three tests un-ignored in `runtime_path_containment.rs` all point their symlinks at targets that already exist, so none of them exercise this path. This leaves acceptance criterion \"Containment holds for a path whose intermediate component is a symlink and for a final component that is itself a symlink, including a link created during the same session by a worker using run-command\" only partially satisfied: the resolvable case is closed, the dangling case is not, and the dangling case is the one that creates a new file (autostart entry, shell rc fragment) rather than merely overwriting one.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","hunk":"@@ -488,6 +518,34 @@ impl ToolExecutor for SandboxedToolExecutor {\n     }\n }\n \n+/// Canonicalizes `path`, following every symlink, even when its leaf (and\n+/// possibly several of its trailing components) does not exist yet: it\n+/// walks up to the deepest ancestor that does exist, canonicalizes that\n+/// ancestor, and re-appends the non-existent tail literally. A path that\n+/// exists in full is simply canonicalized outright.\n+fn canonicalize_partial(path: &Path) -> std::io::Result<PathBuf> {\n+    let mut trailing: Vec<std::ffi::OsString> = Vec::new();\n+    let mut current = path;\n+    loop {\n+        match current.canonicalize() {\n+            Ok(mut canonical) => {\n+                for part in trailing.into_iter().rev() {\n+                    canonical.push(part);\n+                }\n+                return Ok(canonical);\n+            }\n+            Err(error) => {\n+                let (Some(file_name), Some(parent)) = (current.file_name(), current.parent())\n+                else {\n+                    return Err(error);\n+                };\n+                trailing.push(file_name.to_os_string());\n+                current = parent;\n+            }\n+        }\n+    }\n+}\n+\n fn collect_matches(\n     dir: &Path,\n     worktree_root: &Path,"},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","range":{"start":521,"end":548}},{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","hunk":"@@ -202,7 +217,22 @@ impl SandboxedToolExecutor {\n                 \"path {relative:?} is not a safe worktree-relative path\"\n             )));\n         }\n-        Ok(self.worktree_root.join(relative))\n+        let canonical_root = self.worktree_root.canonicalize().map_err(|error| {\n+            ExecutionError::Failed(format!(\n+                \"worktree root {:?} could not be resolved: {error}\",\n+                self.worktree_root\n+            ))\n+        })?;\n+        let joined = self.worktree_root.join(relative);\n+        let resolved = canonicalize_partial(&joined).map_err(|error| {\n+            ExecutionError::Failed(format!(\"path {relative:?} could not be resolved: {error}\"))\n+        })?;\n+        if !resolved.starts_with(&canonical_root) {\n+            return Err(ExecutionError::Failed(format!(\n+                \"path {relative:?} escapes the worktree root\"\n+            )));\n+        }\n+        Ok(resolved)\n     }\n \n     fn read_file(&self, call: &ValidatedCall) -> Result<ExecutionOutcome, ExecutionError> {"}],"remediation":"Make the fallback narrow and symlink-aware. (1) In `canonicalize_partial`, only walk up when the error is `ErrorKind::NotFound`; return any other error (EACCES, ELOOP, ENOTDIR) to the caller so `resolve_within_worktree` refuses rather than degrading to a lexical check. (2) Before re-appending a non-existent component, check it with `symlink_metadata` — a path that has symlink metadata but fails `canonicalize` is a dangling symlink and must be refused outright, not treated as a to-be-created leaf. Equivalently, after resolving the parent, refuse when `parent.join(leaf).symlink_metadata()` reports a symlink. Add tests to `runtime_path_containment.rs` covering (a) `apply-edit` on a leaf that is a dangling symlink pointing outside the worktree, asserting the outside target is not created, and (b) a path whose intermediate component is a dangling symlink pointing outside.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F2-advisory-suite-failure-unexplained","category":"unverifiable_claim","severity":"informational","blocking":false,"title":"Workspace test check failed (exit 101) and its output is not disclosed, so no relation to this change can be established either way","claim":"The `tests-workspace-advisory` check ran `cargo test --workspace --no-default-features --no-fail-fast` and exited 101 with status \"failed\". The package discloses only the stdout artifact's hash and storage reference, not its content, so from the package alone it cannot be determined which tests failed, whether the failures are pre-existing at base revision `2bc5ad81a6e267269e293d07bc9af553d029e3c8`, or whether any of them touch the paths this change modifies. The required `tests-green-crates` check does cover `-p familiar-ai-daemon` and passed, which is the evidence supporting the `runtime_path_containment.rs` acceptance criterion; but the workspace-wide failure remains an unresolved signal that this review cannot adjudicate on the disclosed material. Recorded so the disposition is not read as an affirmative statement that the full workspace suite is green.","evidence":[{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-workspace-advisory","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:64eb2a87e011a4cd49aa79540c8281aa8c23e70ae3f8edc7e073e76d42f955ea","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":111785,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788559145717011-0001981761-000000/PRD-81","revision":"sha256:cc7a993082b189532f4e1573e8ee98f62906b00444e722c0ad0e5e2794cfb151","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-64eb2a87e011a4cd49aa79540c8281aa8c23e70ae3f8edc7e073e76d42f955ea-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Disclose the failing-test names from the `tests-workspace-advisory` stdout artifact (or a baseline comparison against `2bc5ad81a6e267269e293d07bc9af553d029e3c8`) so the failures can be confirmed pre-existing and unrelated to resolved-path containment; if any failure is caused by `resolve_within_worktree` now returning a canonicalized path where callers previously received `worktree_root.join(relative)`, fix it before merge.","status":"open","supersedes":null}]}
