{"review_id":"00001788559361226278-0001981761-000001-cycle-review-2","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:9906d49f7eecac04622f2f00f72cb8d9231666b55baf25b2cb4e1177cc3793fe","findings":[{"finding_id":"F1-dangling-symlink-escape","category":"security_issue","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"Dangling-symlink escape through the not-yet-created-leaf fallback","claim":"Resolved. `canonicalize_partial` no longer swallows every `canonicalize` error: the walk-up arm is now guarded by `error.kind() == std::io::ErrorKind::NotFound`, and any other failure (EACCES, ELOOP, ENOTDIR) is returned to the caller instead of silently degrading to a literal re-append. The ENOENT ambiguity the finding turned on is resolved explicitly — before walking past a component that failed with `NotFound`, `current.symlink_metadata()` is consulted, and a component that has symlink metadata but no resolvable target is refused outright as a dangling symlink rather than treated as a to-be-created leaf. This closes both shapes named in the finding: the dangling leaf (`victim.txt -> /outside/autostart.desktop`) is caught on the first iteration, and a dangling intermediate component (`link -> /outside/dir-created-later`, path `link/new.txt`) is caught after the walk-up reaches `link` itself, because `symlink_metadata` on the deeper path resolves through the dangling link and reports `NotFound` while `symlink_metadata` on the link reports `Ok`. Both cases now have dedicated tests in `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/runtime_path_containment.rs` asserting the refusal and asserting the outside target was not created, and `tests-green-crates` passes with them.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","hunk":"@@ -488,6 +518,56 @@ impl ToolExecutor for SandboxedToolExecutor {\n     }\n }\n \n+/// Canonicalizes `path`, following every symlink, even when its leaf (and\n+/// possibly several of its trailing components) does not exist yet: it\n+/// walks up to the deepest ancestor that does exist, canonicalizes that\n+/// ancestor, and re-appends the non-existent tail literally. A path that\n+/// exists in full is simply canonicalized outright.\n+///\n+/// The walk-up only ever fires for `NotFound` — a genuinely absent\n+/// component. Any other error (permission denied, too many levels of\n+/// symlinks, not a directory) is returned to the caller rather than\n+/// silently degrading to a lexical re-append, because that fallback is\n+/// exactly the lexical-only check this function exists to replace.\n+///\n+/// `NotFound` alone is not enough to treat a component as \"not created\n+/// yet\", though: a symlink whose target does not exist also fails\n+/// `canonicalize` with `NotFound`, indistinguishable by error kind alone\n+/// from a missing file. Before walking past such a component, its\n+/// `symlink_metadata` is checked — a component with symlink metadata but\n+/// no resolvable target is a dangling symlink, refused outright, since\n+/// re-appending it literally would authorize a write straight through it\n+/// to wherever it points.\n+fn canonicalize_partial(path: &Path) -> std::io::Result<PathBuf> {\n+    let mut trailing: Vec<std::ffi::OsString> = Vec::new();\n+    let mut current = path;\n+    loop {\n+        match current.canonicalize() {\n+            Ok(mut canonical) => {\n+                for part in trailing.into_iter().rev() {\n+                    canonical.push(part);\n+                }\n+                return Ok(canonical);\n+            }\n+            Err(error) if error.kind() == std::io::ErrorKind::NotFound => {\n+                if current.symlink_metadata().is_ok() {\n+                    return Err(std::io::Error::new(\n+                        std::io::ErrorKind::InvalidInput,\n+                        format!(\"{} is a dangling symlink\", current.display()),\n+                    ));\n+                }\n+                let (Some(file_name), Some(parent)) = (current.file_name(), current.parent())\n+                else {\n+                    return Err(error);\n+                };\n+                trailing.push(file_name.to_os_string());\n+                current = parent;\n+            }\n+            Err(error) => return Err(error),\n+        }\n+    }\n+}\n+\n fn collect_matches(\n     dir: &Path,\n     worktree_root: &Path,"},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/runtime_path_containment.rs","range":{"start":170,"end":238}}],"remediation":"No further action required for this finding; the residual TOCTOU window that remains (a component replaced by a symlink between the check and the subsequent filesystem call) is explicitly disclosed in both the function's doc comment and docs/contracts/agent-loop.md, and closing it needs openat2/O_NOFOLLOW-style syscalls outside this change.","status":"resolved","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F2-canonical-root-divergence","category":"maintainability_issue","severity":"low","blocking":false,"title":"Resolved paths are now canonical while the executor's stored worktree_root is not","claim":"`resolve_within_worktree` canonicalizes the worktree root into a local `canonical_root` for the containment comparison and returns the canonicalized `resolved` path, but the executor's own `self.worktree_root` is left in whatever form it was configured with. Helpers that relate a path back to the root still receive that stored value separately — `collect_matches(dir: &Path, worktree_root: &Path, ...)` takes the resolved directory and the worktree root as two independent parameters. When the configured worktree root contains a symlinked component (a symlinked home or state directory, or a macOS TMPDIR under `/var` -> `/private/var`), a resolved path no longer shares a textual prefix with `self.worktree_root`, so any prefix-relative computation performed against the stored root disagrees with the path the capability actually opened. The containment decision itself is unaffected — it compares against `canonical_root`, not the stored root — and `tests-green-crates` passes, since Linux `tempfile` roots canonicalize to themselves and never exercise the divergence, so this is a latent consistency hazard introduced by the change rather than an observed failure.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/agent_runtime.rs","hunk":"@@ -202,7 +217,22 @@ impl SandboxedToolExecutor {\n                 \"path {relative:?} is not a safe worktree-relative path\"\n             )));\n         }\n-        Ok(self.worktree_root.join(relative))\n+        let canonical_root = self.worktree_root.canonicalize().map_err(|error| {\n+            ExecutionError::Failed(format!(\n+                \"worktree root {:?} could not be resolved: {error}\",\n+                self.worktree_root\n+            ))\n+        })?;\n+        let joined = self.worktree_root.join(relative);\n+        let resolved = canonicalize_partial(&joined).map_err(|error| {\n+            ExecutionError::Failed(format!(\"path {relative:?} could not be resolved: {error}\"))\n+        })?;\n+        if !resolved.starts_with(&canonical_root) {\n+            return Err(ExecutionError::Failed(format!(\n+                \"path {relative:?} escapes the worktree root\"\n+            )));\n+        }\n+        Ok(resolved)\n     }\n \n     fn read_file(&self, call: &ValidatedCall) -> Result<ExecutionOutcome, ExecutionError> {"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:1959893af052986e242572b2f27074281ab47ab46073dfbc035705b416d2e516","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":76041,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788559145717011-0001981761-000000/PRD-81","revision":"sha256:704117522e19a2abec31071845f764f58ed37613f72861e0fa4e683de3c8b789","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-1959893af052986e242572b2f27074281ab47ab46073dfbc035705b416d2e516-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Canonicalize the worktree root once when the executor is constructed (or store the canonical root alongside the configured one) and use that single canonical form everywhere a path is compared against, or made relative to, the worktree root — including the `worktree_root` argument handed to `collect_matches` — so that a resolved path and the root it is measured against are always in the same form. Re-canonicalizing the root on every call also does redundant filesystem work per tool call.","status":"open","supersedes":null}]}
